--- stdout ---
Move, rename, or re-invite a call you organize

Only what the flags name changes; `--invite` replaces the invite list.

Usage: xr calls scheduled update [OPTIONS] <SCHEDULED_CALL_ID>

Arguments:
  <SCHEDULED_CALL_ID>
          Scheduled call ID, as `xr calls scheduled list` prints it

Options:
      --start <TIME>
          When the call, or the named occurrence, now starts (RFC 3339)

      --title <TITLE>
          The title invitees see (100 characters at most)

      --description <TEXT>
          What the call is about (1000 characters at most)

      --duration <MINUTES>
          How long the call lasts, in minutes (at most 44640, 31 days); X assumes 30

      --invite <USER_ID>
          Numeric user ID of someone to invite; repeat for each person

      --recurrence <RRULE>
          The RFC 5545 RRULE to repeat the call by, such as FREQ=WEEKLY;BYDAY=MO

  -v, --verbose[=<VERBOSE>]
          Print request and response lines, and a note for each key X sent in its legacy post vocabulary
          
          [env: XURL_VERBOSE=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --time-zone <ZONE>
          The IANA time zone the recurrence follows, such as America/Chicago

      --no-notify
          Send invitees no email about it

      --scope <SCOPE>
          Which occurrences, counted from `--occurrence`: X assumes `all` without it and `this` with it

          Possible values:
          - all:                Every occurrence
          - this:               The named occurrence alone
          - this-and-following: The named occurrence and every one after it

      --app <APP>
          Use a specific registered app (overrides default)
          
          [env: XURL_APP=]

      --occurrence <TIME>
          The occurrence's original start (RFC 3339), as a listed call's `occurrence_start_time` gives it

      --auth <AUTH_TYPE>
          Authentication type (oauth1, oauth2, app)

      --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format. text (default), json, jsonl, ndjson (alias of jsonl), yaml (alias `yml`), csv, tsv. Any other value (toml, xml) is refused at exit 2 with a JSON envelope whose reason is `invalid-args`

          Possible values:
          - text:   Default: colored, human-readable
          - json:   Machine-readable JSON, no color
          - jsonl:  JSON Lines (useful for streaming)
          - ndjson: Newline-delimited JSON; alias of `jsonl`. Same wire shape, different name
          - yaml:   YAML document (best-effort serialization of the JSON shape)
          - csv:    Comma-separated values (best-effort flattening of the top-level shape)
          - tsv:    Tab-separated values (best-effort flattening of the top-level shape)
          
          [env: XURL_OUTPUT=]
          [default: text]

      --json
          Shorthand for `--output json` (P2 alias)
          
          [env: XURL_JSON=]

  -u, --username <USERNAME>
          `OAuth2` login to act as, by the name it is stored under: its username, or the name given to `xr auth oauth2`

      --jsonl
          Shorthand for `--output jsonl` (P2 alias)
          
          [env: XURL_JSONL=]

  -t, --trace
          Add X-B3-Flags trace header

      --raw[=<RAW>]
          Emit unstyled, compact output. Strips ANSI in text mode; prints `json` output on one line, as `jsonl` and `ndjson` always are
          
          [env: XURL_RAW=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --no-pager
          Documented no-op. `xr` writes directly to stdout and never invokes `$PAGER`; this flag is advertised so agents can pass `--no-pager` unconditionally without xr rejecting it
          
          [env: XURL_NO_PAGER=]

  -q, --quiet[=<QUIET>]
          Suppress all non-essential output (errors still go to stderr)
          
          [env: XURL_QUIET=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --no-interactive[=<NO_INTERACTIVE>]
          Disable interactive prompts; fail with error instead
          
          [env: XURL_NO_INTERACTIVE=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Request timeout in seconds
          
          [env: XURL_TIMEOUT=]
          [default: 30]

      --wait-on-rate-limit[=<WAIT_ON_RATE_LIMIT>]
          Wait out a rate limit and retry once, instead of failing with `rate-limited`
          
          Applies when X answers 429 and that response names its reset, and the wait fits `--rate-limit-max-wait`. A longer wait, or a 429 that names no reset, fails at once as it does without this flag, and the second response is final whatever it is.
          
          [env: XURL_WAIT_ON_RATE_LIMIT=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --rate-limit-max-wait <SECS>
          Longest `--wait-on-rate-limit` waits before its retry, in seconds
          
          [env: XURL_RATE_LIMIT_MAX_WAIT=]
          [default: 60]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colorize output: auto (TTY-aware), always, or never

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Enable color when stderr is a TTY and `NO_COLOR` is unset
          - always: Always emit ANSI color escapes (still suppressed by `NO_COLOR`)
          - never:  Never emit ANSI color escapes
          
          [env: XURL_COLOR=]
          [default: auto]

      --dry-run[=<DRY_RUN>]
          Check inputs and credentials offline, and send nothing.
          
          No request is sent and nothing stored changes, whichever command the flag is on: a write, a read, or a raw request with any method. The answer says whether the inputs are valid and whether a stored credential serves each request the command would send: `would_succeed`, with `reason` when it is false. `auth` names the app and the scheme the request would go out under, and for an `OAuth2` login the user and whether the token has expired. Whether X still accepts that credential is not checked. The flag outranks `--force` and the confirmation a destructive command asks for, and `confirmation_required` marks a run that would stop to ask. A structured `--output` prints the dry-run envelope on stdout; text output prints the same context without the envelope's own keys.
          
          [env: XURL_DRY_RUN=]
          [default: false]
          [possible values: true, false]

      --limit <LIMIT>
          Global result-set limit, clamped to 1..=100.
          
          Applies to `search`, `timeline`, `mentions`, `bookmarks`, `likes`, `following`, `followers`, `muted`, `blocked`, and `dms`; other commands ignore it. The per-command `-n/--max-results` flag takes precedence when both are set.
          
          [env: XURL_LIMIT=]

      --cursor <TOKEN>
          Pagination cursor / `pagination_token` for list endpoints.
          
          The X API uses cursor-based pagination: each list response carries a `meta.next_token` field, and the next page is fetched by re-running the same command with `--cursor <token>` (or `XURL_CURSOR=<token>`). Threads through to the `pagination_token` query parameter on every `search`, `timeline`, `mentions`, `bookmarks`, `likes`, `following`, `followers`, `muted`, `blocked`, and `dms` invocation; other commands ignore it.
          
          [env: XURL_CURSOR=]

      --page <N>
          Documented alias for `--cursor`.
          
          X's API does not offer offset-style pagination (`--page 2` is not addressable). Passing `--page` returns a canonical `unsupported-pagination` envelope on stderr suggesting `--cursor` instead. Exposed so agents trained on offset-pagination conventions get a structured error rather than a silent no-op.
          
          [env: XURL_PAGE=]

      --after <TOKEN>
          Documented alias for `--cursor` (`--after <token>`).
          
          Threads through to the same `pagination_token` query parameter as `--cursor`. Exposed so agents that picked up "after-style" pagination from other CLIs (`gh`, `kubectl`) get a working flag.
          
          [env: XURL_AFTER=]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  Move a call an hour later (text):
    xr calls scheduled update 1849302018273645 --start 2026-11-02T17:00:00Z
  Move (JSON envelope):
    xr calls scheduled update 1849302018273645 --start 2026-11-02T17:00:00Z --output json

